WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.618 --> 00:00:19.121
In half an hour Sherlock Holmes and I reached the house at 131 Pitt Street.

2
00:00:20.022 --> 00:00:24.746
As our cab pulled up, we found the sidewalk lined by a crowd of curious people, including several

3
00:00:24.786 --> 00:00:25.427
messenger boys.

4
00:00:26.248 --> 00:00:27.429
Holmes let out a low whistle.

5
00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:30.772
"It must be attempted murder at least, Watson," he said.

6
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.882
"Nothing less will hold the attention of a London messenger boy and keep him from his work."

7
00:00:35.682 --> 00:00:39.104
Lestrade was looking out the front window as if eagerly anticipating our arrival.

8
00:00:39.945 --> 00:00:44.567
Before entering the house, Holmes inspected the top steps, which had been washed down with water.

9
00:00:44.587 --> 00:00:47.689
"There is no shortage of footprints," muttered Holmes.

10
00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:49.290
"Ah, Lestrade!"

11
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.391
he said as the inspector opened the door for us.

12
00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:54.192
"It's the Napoleon bust business again, Mr.

13
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:55.513
Holmes," said Lestrade.

14
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:56.854
"What has happened?"

15
00:00:57.214 --> 00:00:57.734
asked Holmes.

16
00:00:57.754 --> 00:00:59.175
"A murder!"

17
00:00:59.455 --> 00:01:00.276
replied the inspector.

18
00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:01.533
"Come this way."

19
00:01:02.914 --> 00:01:06.857
Lestrade led us into a sitting room where a disheveled elderly man was pacing the floor in his

20
00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:07.758
flannel bathrobe.

21
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:10.220
The inspector introduced him as Mr.

22
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.922
Horace Harker, a journalist and the owner of the house.

23
00:01:12.942 --> 00:01:16.546
"Mr. Harker, will you tell these gentlemen exactly what has happened?"

24
00:01:16.926 --> 00:01:17.566
asked Lestrade.

25
00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:21.149
The man in the bathrobe looked at us with a melancholy face.

26
00:01:21.169 --> 00:01:24.192
"It's an extraordinary thing," said Mr.

27
00:01:24.232 --> 00:01:28.436
Harker. "All my life I've been writing other people's stories for the newspapers.

28
00:01:29.312 --> 00:01:33.676
But now that a real piece of news has landed on my own doorstep, I am so confused

29
00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:35.698
and upset that I'm unable to write a word."

30
00:01:35.718 --> 00:01:40.042
"I can't help you if you don't tell us your story," said Holmes as we sat down.

31
00:01:40.062 --> 00:01:42.544
"Well, I'll try," said Mr.

32
00:01:42.564 --> 00:01:47.409
Harker. "It all seems to involve that bust of Napoleon that I bought for this room about four

33
00:01:47.449 --> 00:01:47.969
months ago.

34
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.993
I purchased it from the Harding Brothers store in the neighborhood."

35
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:55.316
He told us that he was often up late working on his stories.

36
00:01:56.264 --> 00:02:00.247
Around three o'clock that morning he was writing in his upstairs den when he thought he heard noises

37
00:02:00.307 --> 00:02:03.510
downstairs. He listened carefully but heard nothing more.

38
00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.254
"Suddenly there came a horrible yell, the most dreadful sound that I've ever heard, Mr.

39
00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.296
Holmes. I sat frozen with horror for a minute.

40
00:02:12.037 --> 00:02:14.659
Then I seized the poker from the fireplace and went downstairs."

41
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.922
Mr. Harker entered the room in which we were now sitting and found the bust gone and the

42
00:02:18.982 --> 00:02:19.843
front window open.

43
00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:24.387
"I stepped out the front door and nearly fell over a dead man lying on the steps!"

44
00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:25.267
said Mr.

45
00:02:25.308 --> 00:02:28.013
Harker. "It was a most gruesome sight.

46
00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:32.896
I called for help and then I must have fainted because the next thing I remember, a policeman

47
00:02:32.936 --> 00:02:34.518
was standing over me in the front hall."

48
00:02:34.538 --> 00:02:36.999
"Who was the murdered man?"

49
00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:37.920
asked Holmes.

50
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:44.184
"We don't know," said Lestrade, who went on to say that the man was tall, powerfully built, sunburned,

51
00:02:44.524 --> 00:02:45.945
and not more than thirty years old.

52
00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.947
He was poorly dressed but didn't appear to be a laborer.

53
00:02:49.748 --> 00:02:51.449
A knife was found on the steps next to him.

54
00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.932
"We don't know whether the knife belonged to the murdered man or was the weapon that killed him,"

55
00:02:56.264 --> 00:02:56.864
said Lestrade.

56
00:02:56.884 --> 00:03:02.226
"There was no identification on him and nothing in his pockets except an apple, some string, a cheap

57
00:03:02.286 --> 00:03:03.946
map of London, and this photograph."

58
00:03:04.906 --> 00:03:07.187
He showed us a picture of a man with thick, dark eyebrows.

59
00:03:08.107 --> 00:03:11.808
After a careful study of the photograph, Holmes asked, "And what became of the bust?"

60
00:03:13.229 --> 00:03:15.929
"We had news of it just before you arrived," said Lestrade.

61
00:03:15.949 --> 00:03:19.250
"It was discovered in the front yard of an empty house nearby.

62
00:03:20.171 --> 00:03:21.291
It was broken like the others.

63
00:03:22.191 --> 00:03:23.412
I'm going over there now to see it.

64
00:03:24.192 --> 00:03:24.872
Will you come with me?"

65
00:03:25.808 --> 00:03:28.450
"Certainly, but first I must take a look around," said Holmes.

66
00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.172
After he had thoroughly studied the room, the window, and the front door, we went in search of

67
00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:34.033
the broken statue.

68
00:03:34.873 --> 00:03:37.475
We found the pieces scattered over the lawn of the empty house.

69
00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:40.917
Holmes picked up several pieces and carefully examined them.

70
00:03:41.617 --> 00:03:45.059
From the intent look on his face, I was convinced that he had found a clue.

71
00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:47.141
"Well, Holmes, what do you think?"

72
00:03:47.541 --> 00:03:48.181
asked Lestrade.

73
00:03:48.201 --> 00:03:52.744
"It is revealing that the thief did not break the bust in the house or immediately outside it."

74
00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:56.754
"He was probably rattled by meeting that other fellow," said Lestrade.

75
00:03:56.774 --> 00:03:57.974
"The dead man."

76
00:03:57.994 --> 00:04:02.517
"Likely so, but what do you notice that's different about this house?"

77
00:04:02.977 --> 00:04:03.518
asked Holmes.

78
00:04:03.538 --> 00:04:08.500
"It's an empty house, so he knew he wouldn't be disturbed when he broke the bust," said Lestrade.

79
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.222
"But we passed another empty house on our way to this one," I said.

80
00:04:12.242 --> 00:04:14.063
"Why didn't he break it there?

81
00:04:14.904 --> 00:04:17.625
Every extra step increased the chance that he'd meet someone on the street."

82
00:04:18.546 --> 00:04:20.167
Holmes pointed to the nearby streetlamp.

83
00:04:20.187 --> 00:04:22.208
"He could see what he was doing here."

84
00:04:23.112 --> 00:04:24.353
"So what are we to do with that fact?"

85
00:04:24.713 --> 00:04:25.373
asked Lestrade.

86
00:04:25.393 --> 00:04:29.115
"Remember it because it may come in useful later," said Holmes.

87
00:04:29.135 --> 00:04:31.896
"What steps do you propose to take now, Lestrade?"

88
00:04:32.636 --> 00:04:35.517
"The most practical way of solving the case is to identify the dead man.

89
00:04:36.398 --> 00:04:39.539
Then we'll have a good start on learning why he was on Pitt Street last night and who

90
00:04:39.599 --> 00:04:39.999
killed him.

91
00:04:40.739 --> 00:04:41.340
Don't you agree?"

92
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.842
"Yes, and yet it's not quite the way that I would approach the case," said Holmes.

93
00:04:46.862 --> 00:04:48.483
"What would you do?"

94
00:04:48.823 --> 00:04:49.463
asked Lestrade.

95
00:04:50.303 --> 00:04:51.584
"Oh, you mustn't let me influence you.

96
00:04:52.496 --> 00:04:54.637
I suggest that you go your way and I'll go mine.

97
00:04:55.457 --> 00:04:57.257
But if you're going back to Pitt Street, tell Mr.

98
00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:01.839
Harker that the man in his house last night was a dangerous homicidal lunatic with a fixation on

99
00:05:01.859 --> 00:05:04.479
Napoleon." Lestrade raised his eyebrows.

100
00:05:04.499 --> 00:05:06.720
"You don't seriously believe that?"

101
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:08.400
Holmes smiled.

102
00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.161
"Well, perhaps I don't.

103
00:05:10.941 --> 00:05:13.042
But I'm sure that my revelation will interest Mr.

104
00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:14.862
Harker and the readers of his newspaper."

105
00:05:15.642 --> 00:05:19.843
Holmes asked Lestrade if he might keep the photograph and arranged for the Scotland Yard inspector to meet

106
00:05:19.884 --> 00:05:21.364
us at Baker Street at six o'clock.

107
00:05:22.304 --> 00:05:24.745
Holmes and I then went on to the Harding Brothers shop where Mr.

108
00:05:24.785 --> 00:05:26.306
Harker had purchased his statue.

109
00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:27.406
But Mr.

110
00:05:27.446 --> 00:05:28.847
Harding was out until the afternoon.

111
00:05:30.287 --> 00:05:34.529
From there we made our way back to Morse Hudson's shop, a trip of almost ten miles.

112
00:05:35.589 --> 00:05:37.910
Morse Hudson was a short, stout man with a red face.

113
00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.132
"Why do we pay taxes for police protection when any thug could come in and break one's goods?"

114
00:05:43.493 --> 00:05:45.433
he asked with more than a trace of agitation.

115
00:05:46.394 --> 00:05:50.035
He told us that he had bought all three busts—the one that had been on his counter and

116
00:05:50.055 --> 00:05:50.876
the two belonging to Dr.

117
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.022
Barnicot—from a sculpture factory called Gelder and Company.

118
00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:57.264
Holmes showed him the photograph of the man with bushy eyebrows.

119
00:05:57.284 --> 00:05:59.266
"Why, that's Beppo!"

120
00:05:59.626 --> 00:06:00.227
exclaimed Mr.

121
00:06:00.247 --> 00:06:04.290
Hudson. "He was an Italian fellow who used to do odd jobs for me around the shop.

122
00:06:05.111 --> 00:06:06.692
He could carve a little and frame pictures."

123
00:06:06.712 --> 00:06:08.273
"Where is he now?"

124
00:06:08.654 --> 00:06:09.154
asked Holmes.

125
00:06:09.174 --> 00:06:11.816
"I have no idea," replied Mr.

126
00:06:11.856 --> 00:06:16.300
Hudson. "He quit two days before the bust was smashed here, and I haven't heard from him since."
